WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.660
Let’s talk about video super resolution.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:13.240
The problem statement is simple, in goes a
coarse video, the technique analyzes it, guesses

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.760
what’s missing, and out comes a detailed
video.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.249
You know, the CSI thing.

00:00:19.249 --> 00:00:20.249
Enhance!

00:00:20.249 --> 00:00:24.609
However, of course, reliably solving this
problem is anything but simple.

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:29.669
This previous method is called TecoGAN, and
it is able to give us results that are often

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:31.470
very close to reality.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.910
It is truly amazing how much this technique
understands the world around us from just

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.640
this training set of low and high-resolution
videos.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.920
However, as amazing as super resolution is,
it is not the most reliable way of delivering

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:50.510
high-quality images in many real-time applications,
for instance, video games.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.770
Note that in these applications, we typically
have more data at our disposal, but in return,

00:00:55.770 --> 00:01:02.210
the requirements are also higher: we need
high-quality images at least 60 times per

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:08.679
second, temporal coherence is a necessity,
or in other words, no jarring jumps and flickering

00:01:08.679 --> 00:01:10.039
is permitted.

00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:15.469
And hence, one of the techniques often used
in these cases is called supersampling.

00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:21.070
At the risk of simplifying the term, supersampling
means that we split every pixel into multiple

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:26.240
pixels to compute a more detailed image, and
then, display that to the user.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.799
Does this work?

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:34.229
Yes it does, it works wonderfully, but it
requires a lot more memory and computation,

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:37.590
therefore it is generally quite expensive.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:43.149
So our question today is, is it possible to
use these amazing learning-based algorithms

00:01:43.149 --> 00:01:44.560
to do it a little smarter?

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.159
Let’s have a look at some results from a
recent paper that uses a neural network to

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:53.289
perform supersampling at a more reasonable
computational cost.

00:01:53.289 --> 00:02:00.930
Now, in goes the low-resolution input, and,
my goodness, like magic, out comes this wonderful,

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.890
much more detailed result.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:07.049
And here is the reference, which is the true,
higher-resolution image.

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:11.620
Of course, the closer the neural supersampling
is to this, the better.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:17.060
And as you see, this is indeed really close,
and much better than the pixelated inputs.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.990
Let’s do one more.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:20.990
Wow.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:28.170
This is so close I feel that we are just a
couple papers away from the result being indistinguishable

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.950
from the real reference image.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:35.640
Now, we noted that this method has access
to more information than the previously showcased

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.580
super resolution method.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:43.880
It looks at not just one frame, but a few
previous frames as well, can use an estimation

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:49.740
of the motion of each pixel over time, and
also gets depth information.

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.140
These can be typically produced inexpensively
with any major game engine.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.780
So, how much of this data is required to train
this neural network?

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:03.970
Hold on to your papers, because only 80 videos
were used, and the training took approximately

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:11.270
1.5 days on one TITAN V, which is an expensive,
but commercially available graphics card.

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:16.160
And no matter, because this step only has
to be done once, and depending on how high

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.980
the resolution of the output should be, with
the faster version of the technique, the upsampling

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:27.710
step takes from 8 to 18 milliseconds, so this
runs easily in real time!

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:32.450
Now, of course, this is not the only modern
supersampling method, this topic is subject

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:37.980
to a great deal of research, so let’s see
how it compares to others.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.780
Here you see the results with TAAU, the temporal
upsampling technique used in Unreal Engine,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.960
an industry-standard game engine.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.050
And, look!

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:53.610
This neural supersampler is significantly
better at antialiasing, or in other words,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:59.430
smoothing these jagged edges, and not only
that, but it also resolves many more of the

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:03.590
intricate details of the image.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:08.460
Temporal coherence has also improved a great
deal, as you see, the video output is much

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.820
smoother for the new method.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:16.290
The paper also contains a ton more comparisons
against recent methods, so make sure to have

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:17.470
a look!

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:23.010
Like many of us, I would love to see a comparison
against NVIDIA’s DLSS solution, but I haven’t

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:27.060
been able to find a published paper on the
later versions of this method.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.370
I remain very excited about seeing that too.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.170
And for now, with techniques like this, the
future of video game visuals and other real-time

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:47.150
graphics applications is looking as exciting
as it’s ever been.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:05:10.700
What 

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:32.740
a time to be alive!

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.620
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

